 "result_text": "I changed `Light.set_light` in `src/uiprotect/data/devices.py` so it always sends the force-on state to the controller. I haven't run any tests or tried it against a real floodlight.\n\nThe cause, as far as I can tell: when Protect itself turns the floodlight on (for example on motion), the library's `is_led_force_on` is still `False`. Calling `set_light(False)` then changed nothing, so no diff was computed and no request went out. The light stayed on.\n\n`set_light` now takes the update lock and applies the change. It then adds `lightOnSettings.isLedForceOn` to the update even when the value didn't change, so the request is always sent. This is the same locking pattern `set_paired_camera` uses.\n\nThis no longer goes through `queue_update`, so `set_light` calls are no longer batched with other updates in a single PATCH. I haven't checked the existing tests, so any that assert on that batching may need a look.",
